% Evaluation order, optimiser transparency and recovery.
%
% Every expectation here is what SPEC 3.4, 6.2, 6.3, 6.4, 7.1 and 7.4 say about
% the program AS WRITTEN: evaluate the way the tree says, left to right, arguments
% before the body, list before count, every step before the next. An optimiser
% (SORT+TAKE fusion, FILTER fusion, math plans) is correct only if it cannot be
% observed, so these cases pin the answer of the plain reading and every host must
% give it on the optimised path too. The order of coercion and evaluation of an
% operator's operands is decided in SPEC 6.2 ("Evaluate, then coerce"; a fused stage
% "costs what the unfused stages cost"), so the operand programs are pinned as well;
% tools/check-eval-equivalence.* hold plain and optimised execution of every case in
% the suite to one answer.
%
% Positions were taken from evaluating the unoptimised tree and checked against
% the "node that actually failed" rule of SPEC 6.3.
%


### name: ord.fn.max-later-argument-error-beats-earlier-coercion
--- note
A strict function evaluates ALL its arguments left to right before its body runs (SPEC 7.1), so a bad later argument is found before an earlier one is coerced. MAX(TRUE, U): the undefined variable, not the BOOL. (LISP-C13; the math-plan compilers coerced each operand as it loaded it.)
--- source
MAX(TRUE, U)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:11
===

### name: ord.fn.min-third-argument-error-beats-second-coercion
--- note
As above, three arguments: MIN(1, "x", Y) reports Y.
--- source
MIN(1, "x", Y)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:13
===

### name: ord.fn.round-second-argument-evaluated-before-first-is-coerced
--- note
ROUND("x", Y): both arguments are evaluated before ROUND checks either; the undefined variable wins.
--- source
ROUND("x", Y)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:12
===

### name: ord.fn.round-first-argument-error-before-scale-error
--- note
Once every argument is evaluated the body checks them in argument order, so a bad FIRST argument is reported before a bad scale. The first argument comes from IF so no host can plan the call.
--- source
X = "x"; ROUND(IF(TRUE, X, 1), 2.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:16
===

### name: ord.fn.round-first-argument-error-before-negative-scale
--- note
Same, with a negative scale (E_RANGE would have been raised by the scale check).
--- source
X = "x"; ROUND(IF(TRUE, X, 1), -1)
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:16
===

### name: ord.fn.power-first-argument-error-before-exponent-error
--- note
POWER twin of ord.fn.round-first-argument-error-before-scale-error.
--- source
X = "x"; POWER(IF(TRUE, X, 1), -1)
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:16
===

### name: ord.fn.round-null-first-argument-before-bad-second
--- note
A NULL first argument (E_NULL) is reported before a bad non-plannable second argument.
--- source
Y = "1"; ROUND(NULL, IF(Y == "1", "z", 0))
--- expect
error E_NULL at 1:16
===

### name: ord.fn.round-bad-both-arguments-reports-first
--- note
Two bad arguments: the first one's error, at the first one's position.
--- source
X = "x"; Y = "1"; ROUND(X, IF(Y == "1", "z", 0))
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:25
===

### name: alias.arith-operand-sees-later-mutation
--- note
SPEC 3.4: a variable reference yields the value itself, so a mutation by a later operand is visible through it. 10 + LEN("10") = 12. The math plan used to snapshot the leaf at load time and answer 3.
--- source
A = LIST(1,2); A + LEN((A[1] = 10))
--- expect
text "12"
===

### name: alias.arith-operand-sees-later-mutation-multiply
--- note
Multiplication twin: 10 * 2.
--- source
A = LIST(1,2); A * LEN((A[1] = 10))
--- expect
text "20"
===

### name: alias.arith-operand-sees-later-mutation-max
--- note
Function twin: MAX(10, 2).
--- source
A = LIST(1,2); MAX(A, LEN((A[1] = 10)))
--- expect
text "10"
===

### name: alias.arith-operand-sees-later-mutation-behind-if
--- note
The unplanned spelling of the same program, which every host already answered 12.
--- source
A = (1, 2); A + IF(TRUE, LEN((A[1] = 10; "ab")), 0)
--- expect
text "12"
===

### name: alias.arith-operand-sees-later-mutation-direct
--- note
The right operand IS the assignment: 10 + 10.
--- source
A = (1, 2); A + (A[1] = 10)
--- expect
text "20"
===

### name: alias.arith-operand-sees-later-mutation-in-sequence
--- note
Mutation in a sequence inside the right operand.
--- source
A = (1, 2); A + LEN((A[1] = 10; "ab"))
--- expect
text "12"
===

### name: alias.arith-operand-keeps-old-value-after-rebinding
--- note
SPEC 3.4's other half: REBINDING the name does not change a value already yielded. The left A is the two-element list; the right operand rebinds A to a longer list. 1 + 1.
--- source
A = (1, 2); A + LEN((A = (7, 8, 9); "x"))
--- expect
text "2"
===

### name: opt.sort-take-zero-still-evaluates-the-key
--- note
TAKE(list, 0) evaluates the list, and the list is the sort, so its key is evaluated for every element: E_NO_KEY, not an empty result. A fusion into TOP_BY(..., 0) skipped the key.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1)) .> SORT_BY(_["zz"]) .> TAKE(0)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:33
===

### name: opt.sort-take-zero-still-evaluates-the-key-two-rows
--- note
Two rows, division key: E_DIV_ZERO on the first.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",0),RECORD("a",1)) .> SORT_BY(1/_["a"]) .> TAKE(0)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:47
===

### name: opt.sort-take-zero-still-evaluates-the-sort-key-expression
--- note
SORT with an expression key: the E_NOT_NUM of "x" + 1 surfaces although the count is 0.
--- source
LIST(3,"x",2) .> SORT_BY(_ + 1) .> TAKE(0)
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:26
===

### name: opt.sort-take-key-error-before-count-text
--- note
The key error of the sort comes before ANY problem with the count, because the list argument is evaluated first (SPEC 7.1: arguments left to right; 7.4). Fusion into TOP_BY evaluated or validated the count first.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1),RECORD("a",2)) .> SORT_BY(_["z"]) .> TAKE("x")
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:47
===

### name: opt.sort-take-key-error-before-count-undefined
--- note
The key error of the sort comes before ANY problem with the count, because the list argument is evaluated first (SPEC 7.1: arguments left to right; 7.4). Fusion into TOP_BY evaluated or validated the count first.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1),RECORD("a",2)) .> SORT_BY(_["z"]) .> TAKE(nosuch)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:47
===

### name: opt.sort-take-key-error-before-count-negative
--- note
The key error of the sort comes before ANY problem with the count, because the list argument is evaluated first (SPEC 7.1: arguments left to right; 7.4). Fusion into TOP_BY evaluated or validated the count first.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1),RECORD("a",2)) .> SORT_BY(_["z"]) .> TAKE(-1)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:47
===

### name: opt.sort-take-key-error-before-count-fraction
--- note
The key error of the sort comes before ANY problem with the count, because the list argument is evaluated first (SPEC 7.1: arguments left to right; 7.4). Fusion into TOP_BY evaluated or validated the count first.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1),RECORD("a",2)) .> SORT_BY(_["z"]) .> TAKE(1.5)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:47
===

### name: opt.sort-take-key-error-before-count-null
--- note
The key error of the sort comes before ANY problem with the count, because the list argument is evaluated first (SPEC 7.1: arguments left to right; 7.4). Fusion into TOP_BY evaluated or validated the count first.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1),RECORD("a",2)) .> SORT_BY(_["z"]) .> TAKE(NULL)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:47
===

### name: opt.sort-take-key-error-before-count-div-zero
--- note
The key error of the sort comes before ANY problem with the count, because the list argument is evaluated first (SPEC 7.1: arguments left to right; 7.4). Fusion into TOP_BY evaluated or validated the count first.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1),RECORD("a",2)) .> SORT_BY(_["z"]) .> TAKE(1/0)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:47
===

### name: opt.sort-take-count-evaluated-after-keys
--- note
A side-effecting count sees the effects of the key evaluation: keys run first (C reaches 3), then TAKE(C) is TAKE(3). Fused, the count ran first and answered TAKE(0).
--- source
C = 0; LIST(3,1,2) .> SORT_BY(_ + (C = C + 1)) .> TAKE(C)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1", "2"=t"3", "3"=t"2"}
===

### name: opt.sort-take-count-assignment-after-keys
--- note
The count's assignment happens after the keys were computed with A = 1: order [1,2,3], then TAKE(2) gives [1,2]. Fused, A = -1 ran first and reversed the sort.
--- source
A = 1; L = LIST(1,2,3); L .> SORT_BY(_ * A) .> TAKE((A = -1; 2))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1", "2"=t"2"}
===

### name: opt.sort-take-negative-count-after-key-error-negated
--- note
Key error first, E_DIV_ZERO, then the negative count would be E_RANGE.
--- source
LIST(1,2) .> SORT_BY(1/0) .> TAKE(-1)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:23
===

### name: opt.sort-take-valid-literal-count-fuses-invisibly
--- note
Control: with a valid literal count nothing is observable, so the result is the plain one.
--- source
LIST(3,1,2) .> SORT() .> TAKE(2)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1", "2"=t"2"}
===

### name: opt.sort-desc-take-key-error-before-null-count
--- note
SORT_DESC with a raising key, then TAKE(NULL).
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1)) .> SORT_DESC(_["z"]) .> TAKE(NULL)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:35
===

### name: opt.sort-take-empty-list-still-evaluates-the-count
--- note
An empty list evaluates no key, but TAKE is strict, so the count is still evaluated: E_DIV_ZERO from the count (the key would have been E_NO_KEY).
--- source
LIST() .> SORT_BY(_["a"]) .> TAKE(1/0)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:36
===

### name: opt.sort-take-scalar-input-key-error
--- note
A scalar input is one element for SORT_BY; its key error precedes TAKE(0)'s emptiness.
--- source
5 .> SORT_BY(_["z"]) .> TAKE(0)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:15
===

### name: opt.sort-take-zero-missing-list-is-undefined
--- note
The spec's own example, through SORT: an unbound list is E_UNDEF_VAR even when the count is 0.
--- source
LST .> SORT() .> TAKE(0)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:1
===

### name: opt.drop-after-sort-key-error
--- note
DROP(0) after a raising SORT_BY still evaluates the list (SPEC 7.4: DROP too).
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a",1)) .> SORT_BY(_["z"]) .> DROP(0)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:33
===

### name: opt.filter-fusion-keeps-first-error
--- note
Each FILTER runs over its whole input before the next starts, so the division by zero at element 3 is raised before the second predicate's E_NOT_BOOL could be. A fusion that treated a bare variable as unable to raise reported E_NOT_BOOL.
--- source
LIST(1,2,3) .> FILTER(1 / (_ - 3) < 0) .> FILTER(_)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:25
===

### name: opt.filter-fusion-keeps-first-error-literal-number
--- note
Second predicate a literal-number; the first predicate's error still comes first.
--- source
LIST(1,2,3) .> FILTER(1 / (_ - 3) < 0) .> FILTER(1)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:25
===

### name: opt.filter-fusion-keeps-first-error-literal-text
--- note
Second predicate a literal-text; the first predicate's error still comes first.
--- source
LIST(1,2,3) .> FILTER(1 / (_ - 3) < 0) .> FILTER("x")
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:25
===

### name: opt.filter-fusion-keeps-first-error-null
--- note
Second predicate a null; the first predicate's error still comes first.
--- source
LIST(1,2,3) .> FILTER(1 / (_ - 3) < 0) .> FILTER(NULL)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:25
===

### name: opt.filter-fusion-keeps-first-error-key
--- note
Second predicate a key; the first predicate's error still comes first.
--- source
LIST(1,2,3) .> FILTER(1 / (_ - 3) < 0) .> FILTER(_K)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:25
===

### name: opt.filter-not-hoisted-over-raising-sort-key
--- note
A FILTER after a SORT_BY must not be moved in front of it: the sort key's E_NO_KEY comes first. (The in-memory twin of the planner cases.)
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 200), RECORD("id", 50)) .> SORT_BY(_["nokey"]) .> FILTER(_["id"] > 100) .> TAKE(5)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:55
===

### name: opt.filter-false-does-not-hide-earlier-sort-key-error
--- note
FILTER(FALSE) empties the list only AFTER the sort raised.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("name", "a")) .> MAP(RECORD("n", _["name"])) .> SORT_BY(_["r"]) .> FILTER(FALSE) .> TAKE(1)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:70
===

### name: opt.filter-after-raising-sort-before-drop
--- note
A third variant: a filter with a comparison after a raising sort, then DROP(0).
--- source
LIST(RECORD("name", "a")) .> SORT_BY(_["nosuch"]) .> FILTER(_["name"] $== "zzz") .> DROP(0)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:39
===

### name: opt.map-error-position-not-moved-by-filter-swap
--- note
The MAP's own error is reported at the MAP body, not at the FILTER that would have followed it after a swap.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 1)) .> MAP(RECORD("x", _["x"], "y", _["nokey"])) .> FILTER(_["x"] > 0)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:55
===

### name: opt.pos.take-take-under-operator
--- note
NOT's operand is the TAKE call; that is the node that failed (E_NOT_BOOL), at its own position, not at a fused replacement's.
--- source
NOT TAKE(TAKE(LIST(1), 3), 2)
--- expect
error E_NOT_BOOL at 1:5
===

### name: opt.pos.drop-drop-under-operator
--- note
DROP+DROP twin: E_NO_SCALAR at the outer DROP.
--- source
NOT DROP(DROP(LIST(1),1),1)
--- expect
error E_NO_SCALAR at 1:5
===

### name: opt.pos.filter-true-under-operator
--- note
FILTER(..., TRUE) under NOT.
--- source
NOT FILTER(LIST(1), TRUE)
--- expect
error E_NOT_BOOL at 1:5
===

### name: opt.pos.sort-take-under-operator
--- note
SORT+TAKE under NOT, with the sort's argument a variable.
--- source
X = LIST(1); NOT TAKE(SORT(X), 1)
--- expect
error E_NOT_BOOL at 1:18
===

### name: lim.eval-depth.flat-chain-inside-map-body
--- note
A flat chain of 5,000 terms inside an aggregate body is E_DEPTH from the evaluator, at the 201st node, like the same chain outside an aggregate. The analysis helpers that pre-scan bodies recursed uncounted and crashed JS, Lisp and C++.
--- source
